// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux Credential Dumping via Unshadow
// Sigma ID: e5b9c3a7-7f4d-4c96-e8a5-6d1b4f9c3e70
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: credential-access, T1003.008
// Description: Detects execution of the unshadow utility, which combines /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow to produce
//   a file suitable for offline password cracking with tools like John the Ripper or Hashcat. The
//   presence of unshadow on a system is unusual outside of authorized security audits and its
//   execution is a strong indicator of credential theft activity.
// False positives:
//   - Authorized red team or security audit activity
//   - Password security assessments by the security team
// References:
//   - https://www.openwall.com/john/doc/
//   - https://linux.die.net/man/8/unshadow
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_unshadow_credential_dump.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/unshadow"
